class MyQueue {

    Stack<Integer> inStack;
    Stack<Integer> outStack;

    public MyQueue() {
        inStack = new Stack<>();
        outStack = new Stack<>();
    }

    public void push(int x) {
        inStack.push(x);
    }

    public int pop() {
        moveElements();
        return outStack.pop();
    }

    public int peek() {
        moveElements();
        return outStack.peek();
    }

    public boolean empty() {
        return inStack.isEmpty() && outStack.isEmpty();
    }

    private void moveElements() {
        if (outStack.isEmpty()) {
            while (!inStack.isEmpty()) {
                outStack.push(inStack.pop());
            }
        }
    }
}
The intuition is to implement a queue (FIFO) using two stacks, because a stack follows LIFO. We use inStack to store newly added elements and outStack to provide elements in the correct queue order. In push(), we simply add the element to inStack. For pop() and peek(), we call moveElements(), which checks if outStack is empty; if it is, we move all elements from inStack to outStack. This reverses their order, so the element that was inserted first comes to the top of outStack, allowing us to follow FIFO order. If outStack is not empty, we don't move anything because its top already represents the front of the queue. pop() removes and returns the front element using outStack.pop(), while peek() returns it without removing it using outStack.peek(). empty() returns true only when both stacks are empty. The amortized time complexity of push, pop, and peek is O(1) because each element is moved from inStack to outStack at most once, although a single pop() or peek() can take O(n) in the worst case. The space complexity is O(n) because the two stacks together can store all n elements.
